Z||000000||sqoach||05-09-2001||12:04 PM||||The cd player in my parents car only has half the lighting that works. It's an older Aiwa w/removable faceplate. Is this something I could fix without taking it somewhere? Buy a little light bulb or something?<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach2.JPG">   <BR>84 SE (project car?)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)<BR>86 SC (daily driver)||||reg||3
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||05-09-2001||03:47 PM||||Usually CD players use surface mounted backlight leds. Only two are needed to completely light the front face. These things are very difficult to replace if you don't have the proper tools.||||reg||1
Z||000002||sqoach||05-10-2001||12:17 PM||||Thanks Cliff. I'm thinking about taking it to Radio Shack (yeah I know, not the best choice) to have them look at it. My brother-in-law works there so he'll probably do it for free.||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroJoe||05-11-2001||11:13 PM||||You have a radio shack that does repair??  I wish the employees at my local RS were that smart.  I asked them to show me their 555 Timer IC's and they had no idea what the hell I was talking about.<BR>   I've just about given up on them really, they carry about .01% of the things I'm looking for, and there is a radio shop just a bit further down the road that does repair on almost any electronic component.  I might as well just go there and drive a little more for knowledgeable service.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
